research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-componentwise-convergence-and-a-vector-valued-integral-computed.prompt.md-1270-- **Steps 1.2, 2.1 and 3.1 are the only work beyond citing the two fundamental theorems.** [[thm-ftc-first-part]] is stated on a closed bounded interval, while $I$ here may be open, half-open or unbounded, so the derivative it produces is the derivative of a *restriction*. What those steps supply is a closed subinterval $[u,v] \subseteq I$ that contains all points of $I$ within $\eta$ of $c$, after which the difference quotients of $F$ and of the restriction agree on a punctured neighbourhood and the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ statement transfers verbatim.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-componentwise-convergence-and-a-vector-valued-integral-computed.prompt.md-1271-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-componentwise-convergence-and-a-vector-valued-integral-computed.prompt.md:1272:- **"Two primitives differ by a constant" is not re-minted here.** [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] already states exactly that, in its second clause, for functions with equal derivatives on an order-convex domain; claim 2 is that statement applied to $G_1 - G_2$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-componentwise-convergence-and-a-vector-valued-integral-computed.prompt.md-1273-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-componentwise-convergence-and-a-vector-valued-integral-computed.prompt.md-1274-- **Order-convexity of $I$ is essential to claim 2 and harmless elsewhere.** On a domain in two pieces a function may be constant on each with different constants, which is why [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] carries the same hypothesis. Claims 1 and 3 use it only to know that closed subintervals spanned by points of $I$ lie in $I$.
